        "Ethical and practical research rules\n- Use public business information responsibly and follow the terms of the platforms you use.\n- Do not bypass access controls or collect personal information unnecessarily.\n- Do not invent a prospect's revenue, ad spend, or performance.\n- Do not make claims about a competitor's results without evidence.\n- Do not send deceptive messages or pretend to be a customer.\n- Respect applicable privacy, marketing, and anti-spam rules in the country where you operate.\n6. Identify businesses with a real marketing leak\nThe source describes auditing five Dallas plumbing companies. The illustrative findings were:\n- Four out of five did not repeat the ad's promise on the landing page.\n- Three out of five did not display their Google rating on the landing page.\n- Four out of five made visitors scroll through roughly three screens before finding a way to book.\nThese are observations from a small sample in one market, not industry-wide statistics. The useful lesson is the audit method.\nThe conversion-leak audit\nA. Ad-to-page congruency\nWhen someone clicks an ad, does the landing page immediately continue the same conversation?",
        "Check:\n- Does the headline repeat or clearly match the ad's promise?\n- Is the service or problem the visitor searched for immediately visible?\n- Does the page show the same offer, location, and key terms?\n- Does the page send the visitor to a generic homepage instead of a relevant service page?\n- Is the next step obvious?\nA visitor who clicks “emergency water heater repair” should not land on a vague page about the company’s full range of services with no clear repair action.\nB. Trust and social proof\nCheck whether the page makes the business credible at the moment the prospect is deciding.",
        "Look for:\n- Google rating and review count, when accurate and permitted to display.\n- Relevant customer testimonials.\n- Licences, certifications, insurance, or credentials where applicable.\n- Years in business, if verified.\n- Real team, vehicle, or completed-work photos.\n- Clear service-area information.\n- Warranties or guarantees, if genuinely offered.\nDo not fabricate reviews, ratings, badges, credentials, or testimonials. Do not imply that a review platform endorses the business.\nC. Booking friction\nCheck how quickly a ready-to-buy prospect can act.\n- Is the phone number visible near the top?\n- Is click-to-call available on mobile?\n- Is there a clear booking or estimate form?\n- Are there too many fields?\n- Does the page explain what happens after submission?\n- Is the service area clear?\n- Are opening hours and emergency availability accurate?\n- Does the form work?\n- Does the confirmation page provide a useful next step?\nThe goal is not to cram every page with buttons. It is to make the appropriate next action easy to find.\nD. Offer clarity\nA prospect may be interested but uncertain about cost, scope, or risk.",
        "Check:\n- Is there an introductory inspection, tune-up, consultation, or assessment?\n- Is the price shown where appropriate?\n- Is the scope of the offer clear?\n- Are exclusions and conditions visible?\n- Does the offer attract the right customer, or mainly bargain hunters?\n- Does the offer make sense for the business's margin and capacity?\nE. Follow-up\nA lead is not a booked job. Check what happens after someone submits a form or requests a quote.\n- Is the confirmation immediate?\n- Does the business call or message promptly?\n- Is there a clear owner for each lead?\n- Are missed calls handled?\n- Are unbooked enquiries followed up?\n- Are reminders sent where appropriate?\n- Can the business see which enquiries became jobs?\nDo not deploy automated messaging without permission, clear identification, opt-out handling, and compliance with applicable laws and platform rules.\nThe one-page audit output\nYour audit should be concise enough for an owner to understand quickly:\n1. What the visitor sees: ad and landing-page screenshots.\n2. The leak: one or two concrete points of friction.\n3. Why it matters: a plausible explanation of how the problem may reduce enquiries or bookings.\n4. The fix: a specific page, offer, or follow-up improvement.\n5. How to measure it: calls, forms, booked appointments, qualified leads, and completed jobs.\nAvoid overwhelming the prospect with 30 minor design opinions. Lead with the one or two problems most likely to affect revenue."
