        "Possible elements:\n- Typical price ranges, if the business can support them.\n- Factors that affect the final price.\n- A short estimator or quote request.\n- Clear disclaimers where an in-person assessment is required.\n- A visible booking option.\nNever invent a price to increase conversion. A misleading estimate can damage trust and create disputes.\nFunnel D — “Repair or replace?” quiz\nThe source describes a quiz that helps a homeowner think through whether an HVAC unit should be repaired or replaced.",
        "A useful quiz might ask about:\n- Equipment age.\n- Symptoms.\n- Frequency of breakdowns.\n- Repair history.\n- Energy or comfort issues.\n- Household priorities.\nThe output should be educational guidance, not a definitive technical diagnosis. It should explain when a qualified technician must inspect the system.\nFunnel E — Owner-led educational video\nFor high-ticket decisions, a video from the owner or an experienced professional can explain how to make a good decision.",
        "A useful video can cover:\n- Common mistakes customers make.\n- Factors that influence repair-versus-replacement decisions.\n- What an estimate includes.\n- Questions to ask a contractor.\n- Financing considerations, if offered and accurately described.\n- What happens during the service visit.\nThe goal is to make the business a trusted guide, not to pressure a visitor into a purchase.\nFunnel F — Advertorial or educational page\nAn advertorial-style page teaches before asking for the sale. It can be useful when the customer needs context before choosing a provider.",
        "Structure:\n1. Name the problem.\n2. Explain why it occurs.\n3. Describe common options.\n4. Explain trade-offs and warning signs.\n5. Show how the business approaches the issue.\n6. Provide evidence and credentials.\n7. Invite the reader to request an assessment or book.\nIt must be transparent about who published it. Do not disguise advertising as independent journalism.\n9. Match each ad to the right landing page\nThe source emphasises ad-to-page congruency. One business may run campaigns for several different intents:\n| Search or ad intent | Better destination |\n| --- | --- |\n| Emergency repair | Fast service page with prominent call action |\n| Routine maintenance | Tune-up or maintenance offer |\n| New furnace installation | Repair-versus-replace or installation page |\n| Cost research | Price guidance or estimate page |\n| Early-stage education | Useful guide or advertorial |\n| Specific equipment problem | Dedicated page for that problem |",
        "A common mistake is sending every campaign to the homepage. The page should continue the exact conversation that caused the click.\nLanding-page build checklist\n- [ ] The headline matches the ad or search intent.\n- [ ] The service and service area are immediately clear.\n- [ ] The main call to action is visible without excessive scrolling.\n- [ ] The page works well on mobile.\n- [ ] Phone and form actions work.\n- [ ] Trust signals are genuine and current.\n- [ ] The offer is specific and accurately described.\n- [ ] Images are relevant and not misleading.\n- [ ] Page speed and accessibility are acceptable.\n- [ ] Form submission and confirmation are tested.\n- [ ] Analytics and conversion events are configured.\n- [ ] The business knows who receives and handles each lead."
