        "22. Protect focus for six months\nThe source recommends choosing a market and geography, doing one thing for that market, and committing for six, twelve, or eighteen months rather than constantly switching ideas.",
        "A practical commitment:\n- One niche.\n- One city or service area.\n- One core offer.\n- One prospecting system.\n- One delivery framework.\n- One reporting standard.\nYou can improve the system without changing the target market every week.\nWeekly operating rhythm\n| Day | Main focus |\n| --- | --- |\n| Monday | Research prospects, review client metrics, plan priorities |\n| Tuesday | Audits, personalised outreach, follow-ups |\n| Wednesday | Build and improve client funnels |\n| Thursday | QA, integrations, testing, client communication |\n| Friday | Reporting, case-study documentation, local relationship building |",
        "Adjust this schedule to the actual workload. A solo operator should avoid filling every hour with sales and leaving no time to deliver.\n23. The find–pitch–deliver operating system\nThe source summarises the business as three stages.\nFind\n- Identify a narrow niche and geography.\n- Build a Dream 100 list.\n- Score businesses.\n- Research ads, websites, reviews, and customer journeys.\n- Identify a demonstrable conversion leak.\nOutput: a qualified prospect dossier and a specific opportunity.\nPitch\n- Lead with the business outcome.\n- Share a concise audit.\n- Explain the proposed fix.\n- Show how the result will be measured.\n- Ask for a reasonable next step.\nOutput: a proposal tied to the prospect's actual problem.\nDeliver\n- Build the funnel or conversion improvement.\n- Test the entire journey.\n- Launch with approval.\n- Measure calls, leads, bookings, and jobs.\n- Report honestly.\n- Improve the system.\nOutput: a functioning asset, evidence, and a clear next action."
